rowt's own two auto-mirrors claiming the same zone — the case that must not
deadlock or loop.

`mesh.example` is BOTH the physical NIC's DHCP search domain (so the corp side
mirrors it into corp-domains.txt) and a zone served only by an overlay resolver
at a CGNAT address (so the overlay side wants it on the proxy bypass). The corp
lane already holds it in its DHCP block, as an older rowt would have left it.

One `corp sync` must settle it: the overlay side runs FIRST and bypasses the
zone (only HAND-ADDED corp entries count as the user's claim, so rowt's own
DHCP copy cannot block it), and the corp side then sees the zone in the hotspot
file and drops its copy. The result is exactly the fixture of
`overlay-dhcp-settled`, where a second sync must be a no-op.
